        "type": "text-delta",
        "data": "I've redrawn the map so it reads at a glance:\n\n- **States instead of hexagons:** each state is shaded by its number of customers. Darker blue means more customers.\n- **Simple, rounded groups in the legend:** under 100, 100–300, 300–700, 700–1,500, and 1,500 or more.\n- **Labels on the 4 biggest cities:** New York (801 customers), Los Angeles (370), Houston (355) and Chicago (267).\n- **Tighter view of the mainland US:** Alaska and Hawaii are still on the map but off-screen.\n\n**How to read it:**\n- **The darkest states are California, Texas and Florida.** Each has 1,500 or more customers, and together they hold about a third of all US customers.\n- **The second tier is New York, Illinois, Georgia, North Carolina, Arizona and Pennsylvania** (700–1,500 each).\n- **The northern Rockies, the Plains and a few small eastern states are lightest.** These include Wyoming (20 customers), Montana (42), the Dakotas, Nebraska, Maine (23), West Virginia (41) and Mississippi (91).\n\n**One line for a non-technical audience:** \"Our customers are where the big cities are: the coasts, Texas and the Great Lakes. Rural interior states have very few.\"\n\nThe legend title currently reads \"Number of customers — customers\"; the tool adds the second \"customers\". Two other options: shade states by customers per 1 million residents, which is fairer to small states, or add a short headline on the map itself."
